WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:03.753
- I had written into the
script this comic book guru.

00:00:04.971 --> 00:00:06.780
In their world, it'd be like a Stan Lee.

00:00:06.780 --> 00:00:09.040
And he's like, "Why don't you
just write it for Stan Lee?"

00:00:09.040 --> 00:00:10.330
And I was like, "I don't know Stan Lee."

00:00:10.330 --> 00:00:11.250
And he goes, "I do."

00:00:11.250 --> 00:00:12.790
And I was like, "Hollywood is good."

00:00:12.790 --> 00:00:13.930
Hey, man, I'm Kevin Smith.

00:00:13.930 --> 00:00:14.763
- Jason Mewes!

00:00:14.763 --> 00:00:16.770
- And we play Jay and.

00:00:16.770 --> 00:00:17.630
- Silent Bob.

00:00:17.630 --> 00:00:18.705
- He remembered.

00:00:18.705 --> 00:00:19.538
- Yes!

00:00:19.538 --> 00:00:23.121
(playful electronic music)

00:00:25.497 --> 00:00:27.652
♪ My jungle love, yeah ♪

00:00:27.652 --> 00:00:29.423
♪ Oh, we, oh, we, oh ♪

00:00:29.423 --> 00:00:32.062
♪ I think I wanna know you, know you ♪

00:00:32.062 --> 00:00:33.200
♪ Yeah, work it ♪

00:00:33.200 --> 00:00:37.040
- I met Jason 32 years ago at this point?

00:00:37.040 --> 00:00:39.830
We both grew up in the town
of Highlands in New Jersey.

00:00:39.830 --> 00:00:43.750
And Jason was a figure
of a suburban legend.

00:00:43.750 --> 00:00:46.310
So people would be like,
there's that Jay Mewes,

00:00:46.310 --> 00:00:48.320
he broke the window at Cumberland Farms,

00:00:48.320 --> 00:00:51.850
or there's that kid Jay Mewes,
I hear he fucked a dog once.

00:00:51.850 --> 00:00:52.840
Which he didn't, right?

00:00:52.840 --> 00:00:53.673
- [Jay] No, no, never.

00:00:53.673 --> 00:00:55.177
- But everyone would say stuff about him.

00:00:55.177 --> 00:00:56.770
He was a figure of mystery.

00:00:56.770 --> 00:00:57.880
People knew him and stuff.

00:00:57.880 --> 00:01:01.127
So one day, he came to my
house and my mom was like,

00:01:01.127 --> 00:01:05.220
"That dirty boy from town is here."

00:01:05.220 --> 00:01:06.077
And I was like, "Who?"

00:01:06.077 --> 00:01:07.780
And I looked out and it was Jason,

00:01:07.780 --> 00:01:09.860
and I was like, "Jason
Mewes is here, why?"

00:01:09.860 --> 00:01:11.010
And I went out to answer the door.

00:01:11.010 --> 00:01:12.430
I was like, "What are you doing here?"

00:01:12.430 --> 00:01:13.940
And he goes, "What are we doing today?"

00:01:13.940 --> 00:01:16.340
And so I kind of adopted him.

00:01:16.340 --> 00:01:17.470
- I was drawn to him.

00:01:17.470 --> 00:01:20.290
They would talk about comics and movies,

00:01:20.290 --> 00:01:23.460
and they used big words, which
is funny now, but they did!

00:01:23.460 --> 00:01:24.341
They used awesome--

00:01:24.341 --> 00:01:25.810
- Me and my friends, we went outside!

00:01:25.810 --> 00:01:27.840
But these guys, they sat inside

00:01:27.840 --> 00:01:29.870
and talked about comic
books and big words!

00:01:29.870 --> 00:01:30.730
- They did!
- We were the internet

00:01:30.730 --> 00:01:31.790
before the internet.
- That was appealing.

00:01:31.790 --> 00:01:33.640
That was appealing to me as a 13-year-old

00:01:33.640 --> 00:01:35.477
'cause I'd be like, "What are they saying?

00:01:35.477 --> 00:01:37.157
"They're saying things like ostentatious.

00:01:37.157 --> 00:01:38.457
"What does that mean?

00:01:38.457 --> 00:01:39.900
"That is magical!"

00:01:39.900 --> 00:01:41.850
And they would talk about girls.

00:01:41.850 --> 00:01:43.860
At 14, I had never even kissed a girl

00:01:43.860 --> 00:01:46.090
and they actually had
a couple girlfriends,

00:01:46.090 --> 00:01:48.680
a couple, but none.

00:01:48.680 --> 00:01:51.030
At that age, they knew stuff.

00:01:51.030 --> 00:01:53.630
So I wanted to hangout with
him as much as possible.

00:01:55.355 --> 00:01:57.470
He had a room full of comic books

00:01:57.470 --> 00:02:02.300
and he had tons of VHS
tapes and movies and stuff.

00:02:02.300 --> 00:02:03.430
So I don't know.

00:02:03.430 --> 00:02:04.810
That's what I remember about him.

00:02:04.810 --> 00:02:06.150
Plus, I had seen him in--

00:02:06.150 --> 00:02:07.450
- Sounds like I'm a pedo.

00:02:07.450 --> 00:02:09.616
It's like, he had comics and movies,

00:02:09.616 --> 00:02:10.936
and I was 13.
- And he had this secret room,

00:02:10.936 --> 00:02:12.310
you pull it.
- And I was out in the woods

00:02:12.310 --> 00:02:14.140
with my friends and he
said come hang out with us,

00:02:14.140 --> 00:02:16.110
we've got big words for you.

00:02:16.110 --> 00:02:17.690
It's a horrible story!

00:02:17.690 --> 00:02:18.740
- He opened this room up

00:02:18.740 --> 00:02:21.040
and there was a game
room, and candy and stuff.

00:02:21.040 --> 00:02:22.133
I'm just kidding.

00:02:22.133 --> 00:02:23.060
(both laughing)

00:02:23.060 --> 00:02:25.160
And plus, he had a car and he drove us.

00:02:25.160 --> 00:02:27.320
He would drive us to the
comic book store and stuff.

00:02:27.320 --> 00:02:28.380
This is what I remember.

00:02:28.380 --> 00:02:29.452
- Our friend's--
- I'd wanna hang out.

00:02:29.452 --> 00:02:30.287
- I know!

00:02:30.287 --> 00:02:33.530
He had a white-paneled
van with no windows,

00:02:33.530 --> 00:02:35.750
he called it the Fun Buggy.

00:02:35.750 --> 00:02:38.120
I don't remember it being fun,
but somehow we're friends.

00:02:38.120 --> 00:02:39.930
There's a lot, I blacked out.

00:02:39.930 --> 00:02:41.370
- There was a bed in the
back, I just remember that.

00:02:41.370 --> 00:02:43.060
(both laughing)

00:02:43.060 --> 00:02:46.460
Think of it as a 13-year-old,
that's what I remember.

00:02:46.460 --> 00:02:47.790
It was super appealing.

00:02:47.790 --> 00:02:49.210
All these things were appealing to me

00:02:49.210 --> 00:02:51.900
because, again, I'd go out
and have fun with my friends

00:02:51.900 --> 00:02:54.120
but I'd be like, oh, I
wonder what Kevin's doing.

00:02:54.120 --> 00:02:55.070
I wanna go hangout with him

00:02:55.070 --> 00:02:57.550
'cause he's gonna show
me "Full Metal Jacket"

00:02:57.550 --> 00:02:58.453
and these movies.

00:03:00.240 --> 00:03:03.770
To me, he had knowledge of
all movies and directors,

00:03:03.770 --> 00:03:05.320
and they would talk about writers.

00:03:05.320 --> 00:03:06.270
My parents would just be like,

00:03:06.270 --> 00:03:08.370
"Hey, did you see the
new Van Damme movie?"

00:03:08.370 --> 00:03:10.617
Which was, again, great,
but he would be like,

00:03:10.617 --> 00:03:11.627
"Did you see the Van Damme movie?

00:03:11.627 --> 00:03:13.667
"It was written by Joe Bob Billy Bob

00:03:13.667 --> 00:03:17.600
"and directed by Scorsese."

00:03:17.600 --> 00:03:19.190
Which never happened, but--
- What you're hearing here

00:03:19.190 --> 00:03:21.680
is that I knew the credits of the movies

00:03:21.680 --> 00:03:23.100
and because of that,

00:03:23.100 --> 00:03:25.720
I was smarter than most
of the people you knew.

00:03:25.720 --> 00:03:26.553
- It's true.

00:03:26.553 --> 00:03:28.810
- So we became really good friends.

00:03:28.810 --> 00:03:30.090
And I think he's

00:03:30.090 --> 00:03:33.960
the most original human
being I've ever met,

00:03:33.960 --> 00:03:36.780
hands down an American original.

00:03:36.780 --> 00:03:39.230
Through most of our lives,
before I made the movie,

00:03:39.230 --> 00:03:40.707
I would always be like,
"Man, you're funny,

00:03:40.707 --> 00:03:43.377
"but I wonder if anyone
would find you funny

00:03:43.377 --> 00:03:45.597
"outside of New Jersey.

00:03:45.597 --> 00:03:47.177
"Somebody should put
you in a movie one day

00:03:47.177 --> 00:03:48.437
"and find out if you're funny

00:03:48.437 --> 00:03:51.130
"to people in Chicago
or something like that."

00:03:51.130 --> 00:03:52.700
And then years later, when I was like,

00:03:52.700 --> 00:03:54.120
ooh, I wanna make a movie,

00:03:54.120 --> 00:03:55.270
one of the first things I thought

00:03:55.270 --> 00:03:57.860
is I'm putting Jason Mewes in the movie

00:03:57.860 --> 00:03:59.790
'cause he's the funniest person I know,

00:03:59.790 --> 00:04:00.880
the most original person I know,

00:04:00.880 --> 00:04:02.080
I wanna see if anyone else thinks

00:04:02.080 --> 00:04:03.780
that snoogen stuff's worked.

00:04:03.780 --> 00:04:06.880
So I'ma stick him outside
the convenience store

00:04:07.720 --> 00:04:08.930
as one of the dealers.

00:04:08.930 --> 00:04:10.590
I worked at a Quik Stop,

00:04:10.590 --> 00:04:12.910
and you always had teenage weed dealers

00:04:12.910 --> 00:04:14.820
hanging outside the building and stuff.

00:04:14.820 --> 00:04:15.653
Never one of 'em.

00:04:15.653 --> 00:04:18.100
There was always a group, or at least two,

00:04:18.100 --> 00:04:20.720
one guy dealing, one guy
is his muscle and stuff.

00:04:20.720 --> 00:04:24.610
And then I didn't wanna
give him somebody to talk to

00:04:24.610 --> 00:04:27.850
because I wanted to showcase
all the stuff he says.

00:04:27.850 --> 00:04:29.110
I'm like, he's way more interesting

00:04:29.110 --> 00:04:30.580
when you just let him go.

00:04:30.580 --> 00:04:32.930
So I was like, I'ma stick
somebody next to him,

00:04:32.930 --> 00:04:36.320
a character who's his muscle,
but don't say anything.

00:04:36.320 --> 00:04:38.010
There's a character in this movie I love

00:04:38.010 --> 00:04:39.920
called "My Favorite Year".

00:04:39.920 --> 00:04:41.680
Herb, one of the writers,

00:04:41.680 --> 00:04:43.670
never spoke throughout the whole movie

00:04:43.670 --> 00:04:44.770
and he would always kinda whisper

00:04:44.770 --> 00:04:46.490
to one of the other writers.

00:04:46.490 --> 00:04:49.170
In the midst of the
chaos of the third act,

00:04:49.170 --> 00:04:50.807
at one point, they cut
to Herb and he goes,

00:04:50.807 --> 00:04:52.580
"My god, this makes me happy."

00:04:52.580 --> 00:04:54.100
And everyone stops and looks at him

00:04:54.100 --> 00:04:55.800
'cause he hasn't said
anything the whole movie.

00:04:55.800 --> 00:04:58.000
And then the movie just
goes back on and stuff.

00:04:58.000 --> 00:04:59.150
So I liked that and I was like,

00:04:59.150 --> 00:05:02.450
all right, man, I could
stick somebody next to Jay,

00:05:02.450 --> 00:05:03.420
doesn't say anything,

00:05:03.420 --> 00:05:05.280
and Jay's just talking
to him all the time,

00:05:05.280 --> 00:05:07.750
and I went with the name Silent Bob

00:05:07.750 --> 00:05:10.260
because I was a big fan of "Twin Peaks"

00:05:10.260 --> 00:05:12.520
and Bob was one of the characters.

00:05:12.520 --> 00:05:16.620
There was Mike and Bob that
lived above the grocery store,

00:05:16.620 --> 00:05:18.350
above the convenience store.

00:05:18.350 --> 00:05:19.220
So I went with Bob.

00:05:19.220 --> 00:05:20.280
It just sounded kinda normal.

00:05:20.280 --> 00:05:23.600
Also, Bob The Goon from "Batman",

00:05:23.600 --> 00:05:25.470
Tim Burton 1989 "Batman",

00:05:25.470 --> 00:05:27.830
he didn't really speak
throughout the movie and stuff.

00:05:27.830 --> 00:05:30.310
So I had to pick a name,
and I went with Bob.

00:05:30.310 --> 00:05:34.300
I couldn't name his
character anything but Jay.

00:05:34.300 --> 00:05:35.240
It never occurred to me

00:05:35.240 --> 00:05:38.190
to give him a fictional name
like Dirty Harry or whatever.

00:05:38.190 --> 00:05:41.190
It's just like he was Jay and Jay was Jay.

00:05:41.190 --> 00:05:43.337
So it became "Jay and Silent Bob".

00:05:44.650 --> 00:05:47.030
- [Jason] Snoochie, boochie, noochies!

00:05:47.030 --> 00:05:49.510
- Honestly, it started off as Neh.

00:05:49.510 --> 00:05:52.630
Neh just started off as
meaning just kidding,

00:05:52.630 --> 00:05:55.270
because at 13, 14,

00:05:55.270 --> 00:05:59.570
I was always kidding with
my friends and/or people

00:05:59.570 --> 00:06:00.500
and I wanted to make sure

00:06:00.500 --> 00:06:01.930
they knew I was joking around with stuff.

00:06:01.930 --> 00:06:03.160
- So he didn't get hit.

00:06:03.160 --> 00:06:03.993
It began as Neh.

00:06:03.993 --> 00:06:05.910
And it was almost like
this weird nervous tick.

00:06:05.910 --> 00:06:08.112
The kid was always going
neh, neh, neh, neh, neh.

00:06:08.112 --> 00:06:09.220
- [Jason] Yeah, totally. (laughs)

00:06:09.220 --> 00:06:10.130
- And then it grew.

00:06:10.130 --> 00:06:12.020
He started finessing it.

00:06:12.020 --> 00:06:16.820
So he would be like,
noitch, and then, nooch.

00:06:16.820 --> 00:06:19.190
Same thing, I slept with your mom, nooch.

00:06:19.190 --> 00:06:20.870
He gotta threw a Dutch on it.

00:06:20.870 --> 00:06:22.760
And then it would grow where he'd be like,

00:06:22.760 --> 00:06:25.660
I slept with your mom last
night, snootchie bootchies,

00:06:25.660 --> 00:06:27.040
and stuff like that.

00:06:27.040 --> 00:06:27.873
It was amazing.

00:06:27.873 --> 00:06:29.320
I watched language grow.

00:06:29.320 --> 00:06:31.830
- I would do stuff for almost
for my own entertainment.

00:06:31.830 --> 00:06:34.920
It was almost like a
nervous tick, like he said.

00:06:34.920 --> 00:06:38.400
I would do things but I would
say it to entertain myself.

00:06:38.400 --> 00:06:41.360
His good explanation is
the story in the library

00:06:41.360 --> 00:06:43.860
when I busted in the library,
we kicked in the door,

00:06:43.860 --> 00:06:46.510
and I didn't look at you
guys, and I was doing stuff.

00:06:48.100 --> 00:06:49.363
- Now you gotta finish the story, man!

00:06:49.363 --> 00:06:50.196
- Okay.

00:06:50.196 --> 00:06:52.330
So he tells a good story
about he remembers,

00:06:52.330 --> 00:06:54.040
and I remember this.

00:06:54.040 --> 00:06:54.920
I would get bored--
- He walked in.

00:06:54.920 --> 00:06:56.900
Me and Walt where at the Rec,

00:06:56.900 --> 00:06:59.670
and we were bagging and
boarding our comic books.

00:06:59.670 --> 00:07:01.689
All of a sudden, it was
quiet, no kids were there,

00:07:01.689 --> 00:07:04.130
it was before the kids
came at three o'clock.

00:07:04.130 --> 00:07:06.930
All of a sudden, the door,
boom, gets kicked open

00:07:06.930 --> 00:07:10.710
and Jason Mewes marches
in and proceeds to fellate

00:07:10.710 --> 00:07:12.870
anything somewhat phallic in the room.

00:07:12.870 --> 00:07:15.760
So he grabs a pool stick
and he's sucking it off.

00:07:15.760 --> 00:07:17.780
He grabs the phone and
he's sucking it off.

00:07:17.780 --> 00:07:20.170
The flag pole, sucking the eagle off.

00:07:20.170 --> 00:07:23.290
And me and Walter watched
him go from item to item

00:07:23.290 --> 00:07:24.350
and he never looked at us.

00:07:24.350 --> 00:07:26.090
It was never like, look at this, right?

00:07:26.090 --> 00:07:28.950
It was almost as if like he
was walking around town, going,

00:07:28.950 --> 00:07:29.783
oh, it's two o'clock,

00:07:29.783 --> 00:07:31.560
I better go to the Rec
and suck everything off.

00:07:31.560 --> 00:07:33.530
And so we sat there and watched him

00:07:33.530 --> 00:07:35.550
and then he reached the Asteroids machine.

00:07:35.550 --> 00:07:38.680
Asteroids, the kiosk, for
those that don't remember,

00:07:38.680 --> 00:07:39.830
has no joystick.

00:07:39.830 --> 00:07:42.180
There was nothing phallic for him to work.

00:07:42.180 --> 00:07:43.830
And we were like, what's
gonna happen here,

00:07:43.830 --> 00:07:45.450
as he stood there frozen

00:07:45.450 --> 00:07:47.250
and then he shrugged and went (babbling)

00:07:47.250 --> 00:07:49.010
on the roller ball, on the buttons.

00:07:49.010 --> 00:07:52.070
So I was like, man, I wanna
hang out with this kid.

00:07:52.070 --> 00:07:54.370
He's a genius and he's
very orally fixated,

00:07:54.370 --> 00:07:56.320
so that's the kinda guy for me.

00:07:56.320 --> 00:07:57.577
That's when I knew,

00:07:59.680 --> 00:08:01.710
somebody should see this besides us.

00:08:01.710 --> 00:08:04.903
He would work for an
audience of two or none.

00:08:05.740 --> 00:08:07.520
It was like throwing away gold

00:08:07.520 --> 00:08:09.980
where I'm like, this is legit funny.

00:08:09.980 --> 00:08:13.310
So years later, after hanging
out for a couple years,

00:08:13.310 --> 00:08:15.210
when I was like, I'm
gonna be a film maker,

00:08:15.210 --> 00:08:17.210
I just remembered all the hits,

00:08:17.210 --> 00:08:18.230
the greatest hits, and I was like,

00:08:18.230 --> 00:08:19.950
I'm gonna have him do this,
I'm gonna have him do this.

00:08:19.950 --> 00:08:22.240
And just to have him be an energy

00:08:22.240 --> 00:08:23.940
and as a player in the movie

00:08:23.940 --> 00:08:27.500
was something that I felt
like, yeah, it might work.

00:08:27.500 --> 00:08:29.050
I just wanna see if anyone finds it funny.

00:08:29.050 --> 00:08:31.380
And when it came out,
nobody talked about us.

00:08:31.380 --> 00:08:33.430
Nobody was ever like,
"Jay and Silent Bob"!

00:08:33.430 --> 00:08:36.820
There's one review that
mentioned Jay specifically.

00:08:36.820 --> 00:08:37.653
They would mention me a lot

00:08:37.653 --> 00:08:39.050
'cause they were like, in parentheses,

00:08:39.050 --> 00:08:41.760
and the director also plays
a character named Silent Bob.

00:08:41.760 --> 00:08:43.590
But they never talked
about "Jay and Silent Bob".

00:08:43.590 --> 00:08:44.960
And then there was a review by,

00:08:44.960 --> 00:08:47.390
I think it was Leah
Rosen in People magazine.

00:08:47.390 --> 00:08:50.187
And she said, "Jason Mewes, you wanna find

00:08:50.187 --> 00:08:52.567
"the rock he crawled out from under

00:08:52.567 --> 00:08:54.587
"and make sure there's
nothing else like it

00:08:54.587 --> 00:08:55.850
"under that rock."

00:08:55.850 --> 00:08:56.830
And he showed me that review.

00:08:56.830 --> 00:08:57.663
He's like, "Is this good?"

00:08:57.663 --> 00:08:59.427
I was like, "That's a rave, man!

00:08:59.427 --> 00:09:00.520
"That's huge!"

00:09:00.520 --> 00:09:03.910
So we had no idea that there
was any sort of awareness

00:09:03.910 --> 00:09:07.360
for the characters until "Mallrats".

00:09:07.360 --> 00:09:09.420
The first time we did a
screening of "Mallrats"

00:09:09.420 --> 00:09:12.853
at the San Diego Comic
Con 1995 at Horton Plaza,

00:09:13.730 --> 00:09:16.367
that was a screening of about 200 people.

00:09:16.367 --> 00:09:17.850
"Clerks" had gotten to home video,

00:09:17.850 --> 00:09:20.670
but we hadn't thought about
that so all of a sudden,

00:09:20.670 --> 00:09:22.720
Jay and Silent Bob come up on screen

00:09:22.720 --> 00:09:25.840
and the audience erupted with recognition.

00:09:25.840 --> 00:09:27.680
That blew me away where I'm like, what?

00:09:27.680 --> 00:09:28.513
How?

00:09:28.513 --> 00:09:29.346
And then I realized,

00:09:29.346 --> 00:09:31.080
oh, because people have seen "Clerks" now

00:09:31.080 --> 00:09:32.540
on home video and stuff.

00:09:32.540 --> 00:09:35.140
So suddenly, we knew that
oh, people liked him.

00:09:35.140 --> 00:09:36.070
- What is this shit?

00:09:36.070 --> 00:09:37.010
Everyone's looking for us today.

00:09:37.010 --> 00:09:37.920
We're ducking Trisha

00:09:37.920 --> 00:09:39.260
'cause she wasn't to talk to Obi Wan here

00:09:39.260 --> 00:09:40.400
about her video setup.

00:09:40.400 --> 00:09:45.370
So I had to audition for Jay in "Mallrats"

00:09:45.370 --> 00:09:48.000
and it was because the studio was like,

00:09:48.000 --> 00:09:49.410
hey, we're giving you this much money.

00:09:49.410 --> 00:09:52.510
And again, he probably can tell it better

00:09:52.510 --> 00:09:55.080
because he was dealing with the producers

00:09:55.080 --> 00:09:56.330
and the casting and all that.

00:09:56.330 --> 00:09:58.770
- [Kevin] Jay had to play
Jay but the studio was like,

00:09:58.770 --> 00:10:01.580
he's your friend, this is a funny role,

00:10:01.580 --> 00:10:03.500
it could go to a real actor.

00:10:03.500 --> 00:10:05.170
So they made us audition a bunch of people

00:10:05.170 --> 00:10:07.693
and I still felt like he's the guy.

00:10:08.550 --> 00:10:11.160
They had him audition against other Jays.

00:10:11.160 --> 00:10:13.260
Seth Green was one of 'em.

00:10:13.260 --> 00:10:14.400
- [Jason] Mark-Paul Gosselaar.

00:10:14.400 --> 00:10:17.440
- He was like, I know
everybody sees me as Zach,

00:10:17.440 --> 00:10:19.010
but what about Jay?

00:10:19.010 --> 00:10:20.220
And we were like, huh.

00:10:20.220 --> 00:10:23.990
But Seth Green, the studio
liked, and Breckin Meyer.

00:10:23.990 --> 00:10:26.420
And Breckin went on to be
in "Clueless" and stuff.

00:10:26.420 --> 00:10:28.660
So there were two guys
that they were like,

00:10:28.660 --> 00:10:31.233
these are the two that
we want to play Jay.

00:10:33.070 --> 00:10:35.860
We did a pizza playoff.

00:10:35.860 --> 00:10:37.730
We had pizza and all the cast was there,

00:10:37.730 --> 00:10:40.530
the studio got to see
all the different Jays,

00:10:40.530 --> 00:10:42.710
the different Brodies, so forth and so on.

00:10:42.710 --> 00:10:44.610
At the end of the day, he shined.

00:10:44.610 --> 00:10:45.970
It was clear that he was the guy.

00:10:45.970 --> 00:10:48.070
But at the same time, the studio was like,

00:10:50.430 --> 00:10:52.640
here, we'll do this for you.

00:10:52.640 --> 00:10:54.270
You can bring him out to Minnesota,

00:10:54.270 --> 00:10:56.000
that's where we shot the flick.

00:10:56.000 --> 00:10:57.410
We're not gonna fly him out,

00:10:57.410 --> 00:10:59.850
we're not gonna put him up
for your month of rehearsal

00:10:59.850 --> 00:11:03.220
so if you want him there, you
fly him out, you put him up.

00:11:03.220 --> 00:11:06.130
And when we see his first day of dailies,

00:11:06.130 --> 00:11:07.440
if we don't like him,

00:11:07.440 --> 00:11:10.230
he's gone and we are
bringing in Seth Green.

00:11:10.230 --> 00:11:12.020
So I said, okay.

00:11:12.020 --> 00:11:13.500
We had a month to rehearse and stuff

00:11:13.500 --> 00:11:15.920
and he got familiar and
comfy with everybody.

00:11:15.920 --> 00:11:17.010
And the big thing was like

00:11:17.010 --> 00:11:18.810
you have to be able to
act in front of people.

00:11:18.810 --> 00:11:19.897
He couldn't do it on "Clerks".

00:11:19.897 --> 00:11:22.743
On "Clerks", the dance scene
out in front of the store,

00:11:24.200 --> 00:11:25.530
I was like, all right,
man, this is the scene,

00:11:25.530 --> 00:11:26.370
all we do is dance.

00:11:26.370 --> 00:11:27.280
Easy, no dialogue.

00:11:27.280 --> 00:11:29.020
We just dance like we always do.

00:11:29.020 --> 00:11:30.900
He's like, I can't dance in
front of all these people.

00:11:30.900 --> 00:11:31.733
And it was literally

00:11:31.733 --> 00:11:35.000
just the DP, Dave Kline, and
Scott Mosier, the producer.

00:11:35.000 --> 00:11:37.300
So those dudes had to go into RST video

00:11:37.300 --> 00:11:38.310
and hide behind the door,

00:11:38.310 --> 00:11:40.330
and the camera was taped down

00:11:40.330 --> 00:11:43.915
so it would just run
and record us and stuff.

00:11:43.915 --> 00:11:45.250
At "Mallrats", it was more about

00:11:45.250 --> 00:11:47.170
learn how to work in front of others

00:11:47.170 --> 00:11:50.030
so you don't clam up and stuff like that.

00:11:50.030 --> 00:11:52.300
And the first day, he was great.

00:11:52.300 --> 00:11:53.470
I thought he was great.

00:11:53.470 --> 00:11:55.570
We sent the dailies into the studio

00:11:55.570 --> 00:11:58.030
and we were fingers crossed,
waiting to hear back from 'em.

00:11:58.030 --> 00:11:59.360
They said, all right, he could stay,

00:11:59.360 --> 00:12:01.330
and then they started paying
him from that day forward.

00:12:01.330 --> 00:12:02.260
- Yeah.

00:12:02.260 --> 00:12:03.403
- Oh my god!

00:12:04.820 --> 00:12:06.323
Holy Shit!

00:12:08.120 --> 00:12:09.000
Aren't you?

00:12:09.000 --> 00:12:11.180
- Oh, Stan Lee, hi.

00:12:11.180 --> 00:12:12.013
- I don't know.

00:12:12.013 --> 00:12:13.600
Everyone calls it a
cameo but to me I'm like,

00:12:13.600 --> 00:12:15.930
he was actually one of
the supporting characters.

00:12:15.930 --> 00:12:17.660
He's in the movie quite a bit.

00:12:17.660 --> 00:12:19.800
And he was in there because we loved him.

00:12:19.800 --> 00:12:23.233
I had written into the
script this comic book guru.

00:12:24.490 --> 00:12:26.827
Jim Jacks, the producer, was like,

00:12:26.827 --> 00:12:28.720
"Hey, man, who is this supposed to be?"

00:12:28.720 --> 00:12:31.777
And I said, "Well, I guess
it's kinda like in their world,

00:12:31.777 --> 00:12:32.950
"it'd be like a Stan Lee."

00:12:32.950 --> 00:12:35.230
And he's like, "Why don't you
just write it for Stan Lee?"

00:12:35.230 --> 00:12:36.510
And I was like, "I don't know Stan Lee."

00:12:36.510 --> 00:12:37.430
And he goes, "I do!"

00:12:37.430 --> 00:12:39.200
And I was like, "Hollywood is good."

00:12:39.200 --> 00:12:41.460
So Jim Jacks asked Stan
if he wanted to be in it

00:12:41.460 --> 00:12:44.250
and Stan said yeah and we got to meet him

00:12:44.250 --> 00:12:46.050
in Minnesota for the first time.

00:12:46.050 --> 00:12:49.820
Me, Jason, and Walter was there with us.

00:12:49.820 --> 00:12:53.023
We were beside ourselves
because this was Stan Lee.

00:12:54.290 --> 00:12:57.220
At the time, comic book
people knew who he was

00:12:57.220 --> 00:12:59.010
but outside of comic book people,

00:12:59.010 --> 00:13:00.770
he wasn't insanely well-known.

00:13:00.770 --> 00:13:04.130
10 years change later,
he would become the guy

00:13:04.130 --> 00:13:06.730
that's the Where's Waldo
of the Marvel movies

00:13:06.730 --> 00:13:08.250
and everybody would
know him the world over.

00:13:08.250 --> 00:13:09.790
Your mom, your grandma would know

00:13:09.790 --> 00:13:11.620
who Stan Lee was and stuff.

00:13:11.620 --> 00:13:15.270
So in "Mallrats", we were able to be like,

00:13:15.270 --> 00:13:19.190
for a new generation that maybe
wasn't familiar with comics

00:13:19.190 --> 00:13:23.000
or who created or co-created
some of these great characters,

00:13:23.000 --> 00:13:24.210
we were able to introduce him and be like,

00:13:24.210 --> 00:13:27.100
hey, here's Spider-Man's
baby daddy and stuff.

00:13:27.100 --> 00:13:30.290
Years later, when they made
the "Captain Marvel" movie,

00:13:30.290 --> 00:13:31.530
Stan's on the train

00:13:31.530 --> 00:13:34.320
and he's reading the
script for "Mallrats".

00:13:34.320 --> 00:13:36.020
It says "Mallrats" by Kevin Smith.

00:13:36.020 --> 00:13:38.490
And in that way, he kinda introduced me.

00:13:38.490 --> 00:13:39.680
He returned the favor.

00:13:39.680 --> 00:13:41.620
Years later, he introduced
me to a bunch of kids

00:13:41.620 --> 00:13:43.040
who don't know who the hell I am

00:13:43.040 --> 00:13:46.293
by way of that little
script cameo and stuff.

00:13:47.970 --> 00:13:49.330
He was a friend, man.

00:13:49.330 --> 00:13:51.255
He loved Jason.

00:13:51.255 --> 00:13:55.090
His last cameo was in Jason's movie.

00:13:55.090 --> 00:13:56.840
Very last, last thing he ever shot.

00:13:58.010 --> 00:14:00.620
Wen Jason shot with him
that day, you can tell 'em.

00:14:00.620 --> 00:14:01.750
- That I cried?
- Yeah.

00:14:01.750 --> 00:14:02.583
- Yeah.

00:14:02.583 --> 00:14:03.590
So Stan, it was awesome.

00:14:03.590 --> 00:14:07.517
Stan came down, I
directed my first feature.

00:14:07.517 --> 00:14:08.350
- [Kevin] Called?

00:14:08.350 --> 00:14:09.230
- Called "Madness in the Method".

00:14:09.230 --> 00:14:10.080
I'm actually excited,

00:14:10.080 --> 00:14:12.950
it's playing in Russia
in the next couple days.

00:14:12.950 --> 00:14:13.800
- You're colluding?

00:14:13.800 --> 00:14:15.070
- Yeah!
- Well done!

00:14:15.070 --> 00:14:16.540
- Stan always jokes with Kevin

00:14:16.540 --> 00:14:18.720
because Kevin put him in "Mallrats"

00:14:18.720 --> 00:14:20.150
and Kevin's a director-writer.

00:14:20.150 --> 00:14:20.983
And he's always like,

00:14:20.983 --> 00:14:22.300
when are you gonna put
me in your next movie?

00:14:22.300 --> 00:14:24.840
So I'm like, well, he
always tells Kevin he wants,

00:14:24.840 --> 00:14:26.280
so maybe he would go on my movie.

00:14:26.280 --> 00:14:27.113
So I was like,

00:14:27.113 --> 00:14:29.227
"Do you think you would be
in a movie that I'm directing

00:14:29.227 --> 00:14:31.807
"and that I'm gonna be part of?"

00:14:32.800 --> 00:14:35.337
His guy was like, "Yeah, he'll come down.

00:14:35.337 --> 00:14:37.507
"For two hours you can have him

00:14:37.507 --> 00:14:39.027
"but he has to be home by six

00:14:39.027 --> 00:14:42.000
"'cause he will not miss
dinner with his wife."

00:14:42.000 --> 00:14:44.230
And I thought that was so sweet.

00:14:44.230 --> 00:14:47.470
But he came down and it was super awesome.

00:14:47.470 --> 00:14:50.760
We finally got the camera
ready and we started shooting

00:14:50.760 --> 00:14:55.000
and we did three takes of the one thing.

00:14:55.000 --> 00:14:57.417
We did a couple more after
but the first one, I said,

00:14:57.417 --> 00:14:58.250
"Oh, can you do it again?

00:14:58.250 --> 00:15:00.410
"Can you do this and say
something different?"

00:15:00.410 --> 00:15:03.980
Then, I walked away and I
got a little teary-eyed.

00:15:03.980 --> 00:15:06.720
So meeting Stan was awesome, first,

00:15:06.720 --> 00:15:07.880
and then seeing him every time

00:15:07.880 --> 00:15:09.520
and him saying hello to me was great,

00:15:09.520 --> 00:15:11.300
and then for him to be in my movie.

00:15:11.300 --> 00:15:13.440
So I had a moment and I stepped away.

00:15:13.440 --> 00:15:16.450
I was like, (crying) this is so fantastic!

00:15:16.450 --> 00:15:18.620
- [Producer] You dedicated this last film?

00:15:18.620 --> 00:15:21.770
- Yeah, yeah, the reboot
we dedicated to Stan.

00:15:21.770 --> 00:15:22.810
He was meant to be in it.

00:15:22.810 --> 00:15:25.790
He was supposed to be
the guy in the third act.

00:15:25.790 --> 00:15:26.960
He was the entire third act.

00:15:26.960 --> 00:15:28.790
He was the dude that
was meant to be onstage

00:15:28.790 --> 00:15:30.913
that our villains are after.

00:15:31.810 --> 00:15:33.920
And of course, unfortunately, we lost him.

00:15:33.920 --> 00:15:37.290
So we had this little piece

00:15:37.290 --> 00:15:39.400
that we had shot on the IMDboat

00:15:41.100 --> 00:15:44.030
during San Diego Comic Con
a couple years ago with Stan

00:15:44.030 --> 00:15:44.863
where I was like,

00:15:44.863 --> 00:15:47.230
"Hey man, let's do some
dialogue from the movie."

00:15:47.230 --> 00:15:49.120
A little kind of audition thing.

00:15:49.120 --> 00:15:52.500
And he was so wonderful in the clip.

00:15:52.500 --> 00:15:55.510
He's not even putting on
the Stan Lee character,

00:15:55.510 --> 00:15:57.930
he just starts cracking
up and just being himself,

00:15:57.930 --> 00:15:59.357
and I loved the clip so
much that I was like,

00:15:59.357 --> 00:16:01.384
"Let's end the movie with it."

00:16:01.384 --> 00:16:02.265
'cause it was meant to be--
- I cried

00:16:02.265 --> 00:16:03.098
when I watched that too. (laughs)

00:16:03.098 --> 00:16:05.093
- Yeah, it's pretty great stuff.

00:16:06.400 --> 00:16:09.830
He's right, hearing his
voice is like a time machine

00:16:09.830 --> 00:16:12.170
and it just took you to
better times in your life,

00:16:12.170 --> 00:16:14.130
or to moments, formative moments

00:16:14.130 --> 00:16:15.798
with your friends and stuff.

00:16:15.798 --> 00:16:18.631
(friends humming)

00:16:24.540 --> 00:16:26.367
I remember after "Dogma",

00:16:27.970 --> 00:16:29.740
we had a lot of famous people in "Dogma",

00:16:29.740 --> 00:16:31.493
a lot of big actors and stuff.

00:16:32.500 --> 00:16:34.880
Unlike "Clerks", all the reviews

00:16:34.880 --> 00:16:37.270
talked about "Jay and Silent Bob".

00:16:37.270 --> 00:16:39.770
And I'll never forget Matt Damon,

00:16:39.770 --> 00:16:43.760
we did our New York Film
Festival premiere of "Dogma",

00:16:43.760 --> 00:16:47.550
went on the stage at Alice
Tully, and during the Q and A,

00:16:47.550 --> 00:16:48.997
Matt Damon was just like,

00:16:48.997 --> 00:16:51.800
"Look, nobody's saying
it, but I'll say it."

00:16:51.800 --> 00:16:54.167
He's going, "Jason Mewes
stole the movie out

00:16:54.167 --> 00:16:56.150
"from under everybody on this stage."

00:16:56.150 --> 00:16:57.630
and the whole crowd went nuts

00:16:57.630 --> 00:16:59.320
and applauded and stuff like that.

00:16:59.320 --> 00:17:02.700
So we went through a lot of
hell, pun intended, on "Dogma"

00:17:02.700 --> 00:17:05.680
with some religious cats and
there were death threats.

00:17:05.680 --> 00:17:07.760
So after that, I was like,
well, the next movie,

00:17:07.760 --> 00:17:10.200
I'm gonna make something that's just fun,

00:17:10.200 --> 00:17:12.500
and hopefully we don't get death threats,

00:17:12.500 --> 00:17:14.620
something that nobody
could be offended by.

00:17:14.620 --> 00:17:17.540
And since they played so well in "Dogma",

00:17:17.540 --> 00:17:20.890
and also, I should give a
little bit of a shout out

00:17:20.890 --> 00:17:22.510
to Wes Craven as well.

00:17:22.510 --> 00:17:26.270
After "Dogma", we did this
tiny piece in "Scream 3"

00:17:26.270 --> 00:17:27.420
'cause that's where I met Wes,

00:17:27.420 --> 00:17:30.400
where "Jay and Silent Bob"
popped up in "Scream 3".

00:17:30.400 --> 00:17:32.603
And we did the Connie Chung bit.

00:17:34.250 --> 00:17:36.880
It was at the same lot we'd
eventually shoot "Strike Back".

00:17:36.880 --> 00:17:38.260
It was CBS Radford.

00:17:38.260 --> 00:17:40.360
We were there for a day and
I remember being on the lot

00:17:40.360 --> 00:17:42.680
and being like, this would be fun, man,

00:17:42.680 --> 00:17:44.880
to be on an actual movie studio lot

00:17:44.880 --> 00:17:46.110
and shoot a movie and stuff,

00:17:46.110 --> 00:17:49.240
and "Jay and Silent Bob", and
Hollywood, that's kinda funny.

00:17:49.240 --> 00:17:52.820
So it all coalesced and
came together and I said,

00:17:52.820 --> 00:17:53.890
you know what?

00:17:53.890 --> 00:17:56.740
Let's do a movie that's like you and me.

00:17:56.740 --> 00:17:58.060
We'll be the leads this time around,

00:17:58.060 --> 00:17:59.510
Jay and Silent Bob adventure.

00:18:01.150 --> 00:18:02.550
He was all for it by that point.

00:18:02.550 --> 00:18:03.770
He wasn't against it at all.

00:18:03.770 --> 00:18:05.310
But there were drugs back then as well.

00:18:05.310 --> 00:18:07.330
That was the difficulty, man.

00:18:07.330 --> 00:18:09.670
Getting him clean used to be problem

00:18:09.670 --> 00:18:12.860
because he fell into heroin

00:18:12.860 --> 00:18:14.633
and Oxycontin and stuff like that.

00:18:17.700 --> 00:18:20.260
Getting ready for "Dogma"
was a Herculean effort,

00:18:20.260 --> 00:18:22.510
and then getting ready for "Jay
and Silent Bob Strike Back"

00:18:22.510 --> 00:18:23.343
was the same thing.

00:18:23.343 --> 00:18:26.250
It was about keeping him away from drugs,

00:18:26.250 --> 00:18:29.447
rehabbing him at the
house, and stuff like that.

00:18:29.447 --> 00:18:32.620
"Jay and Silent Bob" might be the reason

00:18:32.620 --> 00:18:36.170
he's still above ground 'cause
it was always this kind of,

00:18:36.170 --> 00:18:39.670
well, you gotta get clean,
man, or else we don't do this.

00:18:39.670 --> 00:18:41.960
So it'd be something you could use

00:18:41.960 --> 00:18:44.220
to keep him on the straight and narrow.

00:18:44.220 --> 00:18:46.457
But then, the movie would end
and you couldn't stop him.

00:18:46.457 --> 00:18:49.300
"Jay and Silent Bob Strike
Back", he was clean throughout,

00:18:49.300 --> 00:18:50.630
no drugs, but he drank.

00:18:50.630 --> 00:18:53.050
You can see he's got
some kinda booze bloat

00:18:53.050 --> 00:18:54.350
in his face in the movie.

00:18:54.350 --> 00:18:55.410
I remember at one point,

00:18:55.410 --> 00:18:57.533
the guy in charge of Dimension called up,

00:18:57.533 --> 00:18:59.727
he said he saw the dailies and said,

00:18:59.727 --> 00:19:01.890
"Hey man, should we get Jay a trainer?"

00:19:01.890 --> 00:19:03.490
And I was like, "Why?"

00:19:03.490 --> 00:19:04.367
And he's going,

00:19:04.367 --> 00:19:05.200
"There's a shot where you guys

00:19:05.200 --> 00:19:07.230
"are hanging over the camera in a huddle,"

00:19:07.230 --> 00:19:08.063
and he's going,

00:19:08.063 --> 00:19:09.470
"And he's got a bigger
turkey neck than you."

00:19:09.470 --> 00:19:11.527
And I was like, "He's
trying to be off drugs!

00:19:11.527 --> 00:19:14.040
"Don't tell him he's gotta
lose weight as well!"

00:19:14.040 --> 00:19:16.500
So he, on "Strike Back",

00:19:16.500 --> 00:19:18.860
the moment we stopped shooting in Jersey,

00:19:18.860 --> 00:19:19.950
the moment we wrapped,

00:19:19.950 --> 00:19:22.570
boom, he fell back down
into the drug hole again.

00:19:22.570 --> 00:19:24.400
So that was always kinda difficult,

00:19:24.400 --> 00:19:26.170
and probably one of the main reasons

00:19:26.170 --> 00:19:31.170
why we didn't do more "Jay
and Silent Bob" stuff.

00:19:31.320 --> 00:19:33.180
- It's hard for me to watch my performance

00:19:33.180 --> 00:19:36.170
in any of the movies drugs
or not drugs, honestly,

00:19:36.170 --> 00:19:37.920
because I always feel like I watch myself

00:19:37.920 --> 00:19:39.800
and I'm like, oh, I
could've been funnier here,

00:19:39.800 --> 00:19:42.303
I should've gave this look,
I should've done that.

00:19:43.300 --> 00:19:46.090
With or without drugs,
I don't really feel.

00:19:46.090 --> 00:19:48.830
But again, of course,
I know there was like,

00:19:48.830 --> 00:19:53.520
oh, wow, that day I was in the
bathroom every five minutes

00:19:53.520 --> 00:19:55.960
and I kept saying, oh, my stomach hurts.

00:19:55.960 --> 00:19:58.860
So I remember these bad moments.

00:19:58.860 --> 00:19:59.750
But it was always tough.

00:19:59.750 --> 00:20:01.400
I don't know why towards the end,

00:20:02.501 --> 00:20:03.334
it'd almost be like,

00:20:03.334 --> 00:20:05.650
it's almost over and I'm
celebrating now but--

00:20:05.650 --> 00:20:07.259
- 'Cause you were an addict.

00:20:07.259 --> 00:20:09.190
You're just like, I just
gotta get through this

00:20:09.190 --> 00:20:11.380
and then I can get back to
doing what I really wanna do.

00:20:11.380 --> 00:20:12.760
- Jay, brace yourself.

00:20:12.760 --> 00:20:15.177
You're about to meet our lovechild.

00:20:16.210 --> 00:20:19.040
- I think I'm gonna need
to see a blood test.

00:20:19.040 --> 00:20:20.820
- It's almost 10 years now
that you've been clean?

00:20:20.820 --> 00:20:21.653
- Yeah, yeah.

00:20:21.653 --> 00:20:22.830
- And the big difference maker,

00:20:22.830 --> 00:20:24.170
at first it was the podcast,

00:20:24.170 --> 00:20:25.510
we did "Jay and Silent Bob Get Old",

00:20:25.510 --> 00:20:28.400
but then he had a kid, he had Logan.

00:20:28.400 --> 00:20:29.610
That was everything.

00:20:29.610 --> 00:20:33.852
Suddenly all his priorities
snapped into place.

00:20:33.852 --> 00:20:35.852
This would be the dude you'd joke about.

00:20:37.240 --> 00:20:38.960
He wouldn't be in charge of a dozen eggs,

00:20:38.960 --> 00:20:40.270
let alone another human being,

00:20:40.270 --> 00:20:42.200
let alone a small human being,

00:20:42.200 --> 00:20:44.620
but he turned out to be like super dad.

00:20:44.620 --> 00:20:46.710
Hands down, the best dad
I've ever seen in my life,

00:20:46.710 --> 00:20:48.800
and I include myself as
a dad in that equation,

00:20:48.800 --> 00:20:50.723
and even my father.

00:20:52.179 --> 00:20:53.170
I don't know if its because,

00:20:53.170 --> 00:20:55.930
it's because he's got a
million-dollar heart probably,

00:20:55.930 --> 00:21:00.010
but something to do with his
upbringing made him super dad

00:21:00.010 --> 00:21:02.610
because he didn't know
his dad or whatever.

00:21:02.610 --> 00:21:04.840
He's not even overcompensating,

00:21:04.840 --> 00:21:07.760
he's just doing it the way
it's supposed to be done.

00:21:07.760 --> 00:21:09.280
I almost wish he had a kid long before me,

00:21:09.280 --> 00:21:10.990
'cause I would've been like,
oh, that's how you do it.

00:21:10.990 --> 00:21:14.490
And that's kind of where reboot came from.

00:21:14.490 --> 00:21:16.746
We'd be talking about it on tour.

00:21:16.746 --> 00:21:17.579
He's always like,

00:21:17.579 --> 00:21:18.790
"When are we gonna make
another Jay and Bob movie?"

00:21:18.790 --> 00:21:20.287
I'm like, "Never.

00:21:20.287 --> 00:21:22.470
"We did it once and that was it."

00:21:22.470 --> 00:21:23.750
But then I would use it too.

00:21:23.750 --> 00:21:25.947
I was like, "If you stay sober,

00:21:25.947 --> 00:21:28.320
"maybe we will make another
"Jay and Silent Bob" movie."

00:21:28.320 --> 00:21:29.820
And then one day, it came to it,

00:21:29.820 --> 00:21:33.110
and then the story kinda wrote itself

00:21:33.110 --> 00:21:34.850
because he was a dad in real life.

00:21:34.850 --> 00:21:37.320
It was so amusing to see
him be this incredible dad.

00:21:37.320 --> 00:21:40.260
He relates to the kid like I've never seen

00:21:40.260 --> 00:21:42.583
an adult relate to their own child.

00:21:43.830 --> 00:21:47.490
It might be because even
though he's 45 and she's four,

00:21:47.490 --> 00:21:49.430
they're emotionally on the
same level or whatever,

00:21:49.430 --> 00:21:52.520
but it's a really warm,
wonderful relationship.

00:21:52.520 --> 00:21:55.340
And I was like, I'm gonna
do that for the movie.

00:21:55.340 --> 00:21:57.550
So I stole his real life
and put it in the flick.

00:21:57.550 --> 00:22:00.520
- What I loved is that over the years,

00:22:00.520 --> 00:22:05.427
he's adapted our characters
based on where him and I are.

00:22:05.427 --> 00:22:08.270
"Clerks II" is I just got out of rehab,

00:22:08.270 --> 00:22:10.690
and the Holy Bible song
and all that stuff.

00:22:10.690 --> 00:22:12.500
And it's because I literally

00:22:12.500 --> 00:22:14.700
had just gotten out of six months rehab,

00:22:14.700 --> 00:22:16.263
and I had some time clean.

00:22:17.717 --> 00:22:19.840
"Clerks" always super obnoxious

00:22:19.840 --> 00:22:21.340
and you could see it, like I'm young,

00:22:21.340 --> 00:22:24.000
like, I'll F anything that
moves and all this stuff.

00:22:24.000 --> 00:22:25.257
And then "Mallrats",

00:22:25.257 --> 00:22:29.466
I'm a little bit goofier,
snootchie bootchies.

00:22:29.466 --> 00:22:33.590
I feel like it's always
grown a little bit with that.

00:22:33.590 --> 00:22:35.030
Again, it's perfect.

00:22:35.030 --> 00:22:38.513
I think this movie,
honestly, is super clever

00:22:38.513 --> 00:22:41.500
and I aint just saying
this 'cause he's here.

00:22:41.500 --> 00:22:43.420
But I feel like he's super clever

00:22:43.420 --> 00:22:46.360
in how he ties in all the other movies,

00:22:46.360 --> 00:22:49.460
how he's brought it to where
because I'm a dad in real life

00:22:49.460 --> 00:22:50.420
and how I've been sober for a while

00:22:50.420 --> 00:22:51.820
and I'm married for a while,

00:22:51.820 --> 00:22:55.427
and Jay and Bob are grown
up and it's "My Two Dads",

00:22:56.409 --> 00:22:57.970
and then his daughters in the film.

00:22:57.970 --> 00:22:58.913
It's just taking all this stuff.

00:22:58.913 --> 00:22:59.746
- You just gave my secret sauce.

00:22:59.746 --> 00:23:00.579
It was "My Two Dads".

00:23:00.579 --> 00:23:02.768
We literally stole this
movie from "My Two Dads".

00:23:02.768 --> 00:23:05.257
(both laughing)

00:23:05.257 --> 00:23:06.850
- Ladies, Ladies, Ladies,

00:23:06.850 --> 00:23:11.850
Jay and Silent Bob are in the Hizzles!

00:23:13.236 --> 00:23:14.363
You ever had your asshole licked

00:23:14.363 --> 00:23:15.784
by a fat man in an overcoat?

00:23:15.784 --> 00:23:18.367
(Kevin laughs)

00:23:19.424 --> 00:23:21.124
Yo, baby, you ever have
your asshole licked

00:23:21.124 --> 00:23:24.133
by a fat man in an overcoat?

00:23:24.133 --> 00:23:25.200
Yeah!

00:23:25.200 --> 00:23:27.850
- The entire series, that's
the highlight for you?

00:23:27.850 --> 00:23:29.050
- It's one of 'em, yeah.

00:23:30.230 --> 00:23:33.650
On the screen, but meeting
Stan, if we're going back,

00:23:33.650 --> 00:23:36.400
that is one of my favorite in "Mallrats".

00:23:36.400 --> 00:23:37.233
But that's my favorite.

00:23:37.233 --> 00:23:38.066
- Your character never met Stan.

00:23:38.066 --> 00:23:38.899
- No, I know.

00:23:38.899 --> 00:23:40.110
That's why I'm saying my character,

00:23:40.110 --> 00:23:41.490
this is my favorite moment.

00:23:41.490 --> 00:23:42.930
- Still!

00:23:42.930 --> 00:23:44.453
All those movies, it comes down to this?

00:23:44.453 --> 00:23:46.240
That would irritate me

00:23:46.240 --> 00:23:47.690
when people would talk about "Clerks",

00:23:47.690 --> 00:23:48.620
like, oh, my favorite part

00:23:48.620 --> 00:23:50.230
is when you guys dance in the dark.

00:23:50.230 --> 00:23:52.630
And I'm like, nobody says anything!

00:23:52.630 --> 00:23:54.030
That movie's all about dialogue!

00:23:54.030 --> 00:23:55.760
It was always like a weird
backhanded compliment.

00:23:55.760 --> 00:23:59.550
My favorite moment in any
of the movies hands down

00:23:59.550 --> 00:24:03.380
belongs to him and Harley.

00:24:03.380 --> 00:24:07.310
They have this kind of quasi
breakup moment in the movie

00:24:07.310 --> 00:24:09.420
where they have to go their separate ways.

00:24:09.420 --> 00:24:11.870
And the two of 'em were
absolutely dazzling.

00:24:11.870 --> 00:24:14.860
I cry every time I watch it,

00:24:14.860 --> 00:24:17.630
and I honestly believed
for those few seconds

00:24:17.630 --> 00:24:19.720
that they're not the two people I know.

00:24:19.720 --> 00:24:21.500
They transcend with their performance,

00:24:21.500 --> 00:24:24.240
and I forget she's my kid
and I forget he's him,

00:24:24.240 --> 00:24:26.420
and I go like, these poor people,

00:24:26.420 --> 00:24:28.623
if they could just get
their shit together.

00:24:29.580 --> 00:24:31.230
She needs a dad, he needs a daughter.

00:24:31.230 --> 00:24:32.650
Oh, it makes me upset even now.

00:24:32.650 --> 00:24:34.560
Not upset, but like happy tears.

00:24:34.560 --> 00:24:35.950
And it was him and her.

00:24:35.950 --> 00:24:38.050
They blew the doors off that moment.

00:24:38.050 --> 00:24:40.253
And of everything I've ever
done, that stands out for me.

00:24:40.253 --> 00:24:43.253
(upbeat drum music)

